(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-vignettes"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 59-year-old man with three coronary stents and no valve disease needs a tooth extracted. He asks whether he needs antibiotics before the procedure. Which advice is most accurate?","choices":["No prophylaxis is needed","Amoxicillin before every visit","Clindamycin before the visit","Postpone until stents removed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prophylaxis is reserved for high-risk conditions (prosthetic valves or valve-repair material, previous endocarditis, certain congenital heart disease, cardiac transplant with valve regurgitation); coronary stents are not among them. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve needs a dental extraction. He had a mild delayed rash (no hives, swelling or anaphylaxis) with penicillin and cannot take oral medication. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral azithromycin","Clindamycin injection","Cefazolin by injection","Ampicillin injection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin-allergic patients unable to take oral medication receive cefazolin or ceftriaxone intramuscularly or intravenously, provided there is no history of anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with native valve endocarditis is improving on intravenous antibiotics and asks whether he can switch to pills so he can go home sooner. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["Therapy stays parenteral","Pills work just as well now","Two more days, then stop","Only if the fever returns"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics for endocarditis should be given parenterally and must be bactericidal and prolonged, typically 4-6 weeks from the first negative culture. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman is brought in by her daughter for 2 days of new confusion. She has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), a new murmur and conjunctival petechiae (pinpoint hemorrhages on the conjunctiva). Brain imaging shows multiple small scattered lesions without a large stroke. Which mechanism best explains her confusion?","choices":["Low-flow ischemia from heart block","A side effect of sedation","Septic emboli to the brain","A primary bacterial meningitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vegetations shower septic emboli into the systemic circulation; the brain is affected (ischemic stroke, abscess, hemorrhage, meningitis), producing neurologic deficits such as confusion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has 2 months of fever, weight loss and fatigue. A new murmur with a tumor plop (a low-pitched early diastolic sound) is heard, and three sets of blood cultures drawn before any antibiotics are negative. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Subacute endocarditis","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Lyme disease","Atrial myxoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrial myxoma mimics endocarditis with fever and a new murmur, a tumor plop or mid-diastolic rumble, but it is a tumor and the cultures stay negative. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a malar rash, painful joints and a positive antinuclear antibody test has a new murmur. She has no fever, repeated blood cultures are negative, and echocardiography shows small sterile-appearing valve vegetations. Which is the most likely cause of her valve lesion?","choices":["Device endocarditis","Libman-Sacks endocarditis","Subacute infective endocarditis","Acute infective endocarditis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic lupus erythematosus may present with non-infective (Libman-Sacks) endocarditis, which mimics infective endocarditis in the autoimmune differential. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with mitral valve endocarditis has 2 days of worsening breathlessness and orthopnea (breathlessness when lying flat). A new, loud regurgitant murmur is heard and crackles fill both lung bases. Which mechanism explains her heart failure?","choices":["A toxic myocarditis from fever","Fluid overload from antibiotics","Constriction from the pericardium","Valvular dysfunction from infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Congestive heart failure in infective endocarditis is due to valvular dysfunction, here from leaflet damage or chordal rupture, and it is the most common indication for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has enterococcal endocarditis of the aortic valve after recurrent urinary infections. Which outcome is he at notable risk for?","choices":["Septic lung emboli","Needing valve replacement","Spontaneous cure","Lowest death rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enterococcal endocarditis is difficult to treat because of antibiotic resistance, and major complications with need for valve replacement occur in about 30-40%. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man has had positive blood cultures for 10 days despite an antibiotic regimen that matches his organism's sensitivities, and he still has fevers. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Switch to oral therapy","Stop antibiotics and observe","Start full anticoagulation","Evaluation for surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Infection not resolving with appropriate antibiotic therapy is an indication for surgery, as are heart failure from valve dysfunction, abscess and fungal infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with subacute endocarditis has a fundoscopic examination that shows Roth spots (white-centered retinal hemorrhages). What process causes these spots?","choices":["Vasculitis of small retinal arteries","Retinal detachment from trauma","Optic nerve swelling from pressure","Hypertensive retinal changes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Roth spots are white-centered hemorrhages from vasculitis affecting the small arteries of the retina, grouped with the immunologic phenomena in the Duke criteria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with native aortic valve endocarditis and a large vegetation asks whether a blood thinner would prevent a stroke. He has no other reason for anticoagulation. Which choice should be avoided?","choices":["Consulting infectious disease","Starting full anticoagulation","Continuing his antibiotics","Monitoring renal function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation is not beneficial in patients with native valve infection (it is controversial with prosthetic valves), so it should not be started for this reason. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a fever, a new murmur and Janeway lesions (painless macules on her soles), and two blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Her transesophageal echocardiogram is read as normal. Which conclusion is correct?","choices":["Endocarditis is not ruled out","Endocarditis is now excluded","The cultures were contaminated","She has only a skin infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal transesophageal echocardiogram does not necessarily rule out endocarditis; with a typical organism and supporting signs she is still treated as having it. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man who injects drugs has a fever of 39.4 °C (102.9 °F) and Janeway lesions (painless hemorrhagic macules) on the soles of both feet. Blood cultures are pending. Under the modified Duke criteria, which category do the Janeway lesions fall into?","choices":["Immunologic phenomenon","Major endocardial criterion","Microbiologic evidence","Vascular phenomenon"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Janeway lesions are a vascular (embolic) phenomenon, a minor criterion with major arterial emboli, septic pulmonary infarcts, mycotic aneurysm, intracranial hemorrhage and conjunctival hemorrhages. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis needs a root canal. She developed hives (urticaria) after amoxicillin years ago and can take pills. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral amoxicillin","Oral clindamycin","Oral azithromycin","Oral cephalexin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A penicillin-allergic patient who can take oral medication gets azithromycin or clarithromycin, or doxycycline; cephalexin is avoided after urticaria to penicillin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with fever, a new murmur and positive blood cultures is stable. A transthoracic study is limited by his body habitus. Which imaging test best detects vegetations and defines the extent of disease?","choices":["Transesophageal echocardiogram","Repeat transthoracic study","Chest radiograph","Coronary angiography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Transesophageal echocardiography is highly sensitive and specific, detects vegetations on the valve leaflets and defines the extent of disease. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with a prosthetic valve and a past episode of anaphylaxis to penicillin needs a dental cleaning and can take pills. Which prophylactic agent should be avoided?","choices":["Clarithromycin","Doxycycline","Cephalexin","Azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin, cefazolin and ceftriaxone should not be used in patients with a history of anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria to penicillin or ampicillin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"An 85-year-old man with degenerative aortic valve disease presents with fever and a new murmur. His family is surprised that a heart infection could occur at his age. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["It occurs only in drug users","It occurs only in children","Age lowers the risk","Over-60s are over half of cases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk is increased among the elderly: patients over 60 account for more than 50% of cases, and degenerative valve disease is now a leading predisposing condition. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with mitral valve endocarditis develops worsening breathlessness, crackles and a new loud regurgitant murmur on day 3 of appropriate antibiotics. Echocardiography shows a ruptured chord with severe regurgitation. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Outpatient cardiology","Cardiothoracic surgery","Addiction medicine","Palliative care alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure due to valvular dysfunction is the most common indication for surgery, and cardiothoracic surgery evaluates the need for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man who injects drugs has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F) and Roth spots (white-centered retinal hemorrhages) on fundoscopy, meeting three minor Duke criteria. He has received no antibiotics. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order a chest radiograph and discharge","Draw blood cultures from separate sites","Start oral amoxicillin and recheck later","Reassure him that it is not endocarditis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blood cultures (three sets from separate sites) come before any antibiotic; a positive culture with a typical organism would add a major criterion and make the case definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman in urgent care has a fever, a new murmur and splinter hemorrhages (dark lines under the nail plates). Before transfer, a colleague proposes giving a dose of ceftriaxone right away. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Drawing three sets of cultures","Arranging hospital admission","Requesting an echocardiogram","Giving antibiotics before cultures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy is started only after blood cultures have been drawn; antibiotics given first may sterilize the cultures and lose the organism. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with a prosthetic mitral valve has a fever of 38.7 °C (101.7 °F). Three blood cultures grow viridans streptococci, and a transesophageal echocardiogram shows new partial dehiscence of the prosthesis. How many major Duke criteria does he meet?","choices":["No major criteria","Three major criteria","Two major criteria","One major criterion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A typical organism in separate cultures is one major criterion, and new partial dehiscence of a prosthetic valve on echocardiogram is the second; two major criteria make the case definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 4-month-old with tachycardia, poor feeding, respiratory distress and hypotension is being worked up for sepsis. A new murmur is heard. Why must endocarditis be considered even though the presentation looks like sepsis?","choices":["Infant endocarditis mimics sepsis","Infants show classic Osler nodes","Infants always have joint pain","Sepsis never causes tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In neonates and infants endocarditis presents nonspecifically and is often indistinguishable from sepsis or heart failure: feeding difficulty, respiratory distress, tachycardia, hypotension and a new or changing murmur. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with native mitral valve endocarditis after dental work has blood cultures growing penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci. Which antibiotic is a directed choice?","choices":["Nafcillin or oxacillin","Rifampin plus gentamicin","Daptomycin alone","Penicillin G or ceftriaxone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococci are treated with penicillin G or ceftriaxone, with or without gentamicin, or vancomycin if penicillin cannot be used. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had his aortic valve replaced 6 weeks ago. He now has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F) and a new regurgitant murmur, and transesophageal imaging shows a vegetation on the prosthesis. Which organisms are most likely?","choices":["Enterococci from urine","Airway gram-negatives","Staphylococci from the skin","Viridans streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early prosthetic valve endocarditis is caused by coagulase-negative staphylococci (Staphylococcus epidermidis) and Staphylococcus aureus, skin organisms introduced around the time of surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has 3 weeks of low-grade fever and malaise that began a week after a dental extraction. A new murmur of mitral regurgitation is heard. Three sets of blood cultures are drawn. Which organism are they most likely to grow?","choices":["Candida species","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Enterococci"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The oral cavity is the source of viridans streptococci, the classic cause of subacute endocarditis after dental procedures and the organism dental prophylaxis targets. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has had 2 months of low-grade fevers to 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), fatigue and a 4-kilogram weight loss. She had a tooth extracted 10 weeks ago. A new apical systolic murmur is heard, and splinter hemorrhages (thin dark lines under the nail plates) are seen on two fingers. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute infective endocarditis","Subacute infective endocarditis","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Polymyalgia rheumatica"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An indolent course with lower fevers, malaise and weight loss over weeks to months, a new murmur, splinter hemorrhages and a dental source fits subacute endocarditis, classically from viridans streptococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who injects drugs has 5 days of high fever and rigors. Transthoracic imaging shows a large vegetation on the tricuspid valve, and three sets of blood cultures are drawn from separate sites. Which organism are the cultures most likely to grow?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci","Enterococcus faecalis","Candida albicans"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs, Staphylococcus aureus causes more than 60% of all endocarditis and 80-90% of cases where the tricuspid valve is infected, so methicillin resistance must be assumed until proven otherwise. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 4-year-old with cyanotic congenital heart disease and a systemic-to-pulmonary shunt has 2 weeks of fever. The murmur sounds unchanged. Which finding would most suggest that the shunt is infected?","choices":["Osler nodes on fingers","Rising blood pressure","Falling oxygen saturation","An unchanged murmur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In children with cyanotic congenital heart disease and systemic-pulmonary shunts the murmur may not change; falling oxygen saturation can signal graft infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man who injects drugs has a fever of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F) and Janeway lesions (painless macules) on his palms. A transesophageal echocardiogram shows an oscillating mass on the tricuspid valve. Blood cultures are still pending. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Definite endocarditis","Possible endocarditis","Rejected endocarditis","Not classifiable yet"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One major criterion (the oscillating mass on echocardiogram) plus three minor criteria (injection drug use as predisposition, fever, and Janeway lesions as a vascular phenomenon) make endocarditis definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 12-year-old had a ventricular septal defect closed with a prosthetic patch 4 months ago and is now scheduled for a dental extraction. Which is the most appropriate step regarding endocarditis?","choices":["Give antibiotics afterward","Give prophylaxis beforehand","No prophylaxis is needed","Delay the extraction a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The first 6 months after congenital heart disease repair with prosthetic material or a device is a high-risk period, and extraction manipulates the gums, so prophylaxis is given. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
